1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Let's build a simple AI agent using Microsoft Foundry. I'll head over to the portal to get started.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:16,350
Here's my list of existing projects. To start fresh, I'll click 'Create project' in the top right.

3
00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:27,000
I'll give the project a name — Foundry suggests one automatically, but you can change it. Under Advanced options, I'll choose a resource group and region.

4
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Once that's set, I'll click Create.

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Foundry takes a moment to provision the project's resources behind the scenes.

6
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:11,690
And we're in — this is the project home screen. I've got two paths: Build an agent, no code needed, or Code an agent for full control. I'll go with Build an agent.

7
00:01:11,690 --> 00:01:21,190
This opens a quick dialog asking for a name — this becomes the agent's identifier. I'll keep the default Foundry generated, and click Create.

8
00:01:21,190 --> 00:01:57,000
Foundry checks model capacity for a moment, and there's the agent builder. On the left: the model — already set to GPT-5 — plus Instructions, Tools, Knowledge, and Memory. On the right, a live chat panel to test the agent as I build it.

9
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Let's start with Instructions — this tells the agent what it is and what it should do.

10
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:27,000
I'll write: 'You are the AI Animationz assistant. Your role is to help users understand and create AI-generated animations.'

11
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Foundry already includes a Web search tool by default, and I can add more from here — for a simple agent, the defaults are enough.

12
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:57,000
Time to test it. I'll ask: 'hi what you can help me with' — and in a few seconds, I get a full response, listing exactly what it can help with and even offering a structured format to get started.

13
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:07,000
That's the whole flow — create a project, build an agent, write instructions, and test it right in the playground. No code required.
